From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Route thread creation and sleeping through the seams

The synchronisation seam added by the previous patch covered mutex and
lock_guard. It missed thread, and the omission is worth recording because of how
it was found.

The subset checker reads #include lines. librecomp never includes <thread>
directly -- it arrives transitively -- so nothing complained while recomp.cpp
went on constructing the game thread with std::thread. The binary linked, loaded
on Windows 95, and died at startup:

    terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::system_error'
      what():  Resource temporarily unavailable

That is pthread_create failing behind the standard library. A guard that reads
includes cannot see a use; the checker now watches the types themselves, which
is what it already does for filesystem operations.

Four sites move: the game thread in recomp.cpp, the saving thread in pi.cpp, and
the mod configuration thread in mods.cpp and mods.hpp. mods.cpp's nine
unique_locks come with them.

Sleeping moves too. ultramodern::sleep_milliseconds and sleep_until called
std::this_thread, as did vi.cpp's spin on the VI counter, and each would have
pulled <thread> in on its own. The alias is named platform_thread rather than
this_thread: ultramodern already has a function by that name returning the
current OSThread, and the collision is a compile error -- which is how it was
found.

---

--- a/librecomp/include/librecomp/sync.hpp
+++ b/librecomp/include/librecomp/sync.hpp
@@ -22,9 +22,17 @@
 //     LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_SYNC_HEADER   header to include, as a string
 //     LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_SYNC_NS       namespace providing the types
 //
-// It must provide mutex, and lock_guard usable both with and without an
-// explicit template argument. That is all librecomp uses: recomp.cpp and
-// pi.cpp guard shared tables, and never wait on a condition or start a thread.
+// It must provide mutex, lock_guard usable both with and without an explicit
+// template argument, and thread with default construction, a variadic
+// constructor, move assignment, join and joinable.
+//
+// thread was missing from the first version of this header, and the omission is
+// worth recording. The subset checker reads #include lines, and librecomp never
+// includes <thread> directly -- it arrives transitively -- so nothing complained
+// while recomp.cpp went on constructing the game thread with std::thread. The
+// binary loaded on Windows 95 and died at startup with std::system_error,
+// "Resource temporarily unavailable": pthread_create failing behind the
+// standard library. A guard that reads includes cannot see a use.
 //
 // Defining neither macro -- the default, and what every existing target does --
 // leaves the standard library in place, so this header changes nothing for them.
@@ -39,6 +47,10 @@
 
 namespace recomp::sync {
     using mutex = LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_SYNC_NS::mutex;
+    using thread = LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_SYNC_NS::thread;
+
+    template <typename M>
+    using unique_lock = LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_SYNC_NS::unique_lock<M>;
 
     template <typename M>
     using lock_guard = LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_SYNC_NS::lock_guard<M>;
@@ -54,10 +66,18 @@
 //
 // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: modern-target branch, never compiled on Windows 95
 #include <mutex>
+// DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: modern-target branch, never compiled on Windows 95
+#include <thread>
 
 namespace recomp::sync {
+    // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: modern-target branch, never compiled on Windows 95
     using std::lock_guard;
+    // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: modern-target branch, never compiled on Windows 95
     using std::mutex;
+    // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: modern-target branch, never compiled on Windows 95
+    using std::thread;
+    // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: modern-target branch, never compiled on Windows 95
+    using std::unique_lock;
 }
 
 #endif
